ctively computable constants C and L. We will refer to this last in\u0002equality as Linnik’s theorem. The following table, taken from [8, p. 266] and\nL10@P0: supplemented by three additional references, lists some proven admissible\nL11@P0: values for L.\nL12@P0: Table 1. Admissible values for L\nL13@P0: L Year of publication Author Reference\nL14@P0: 10000 1957 Pan [15]\nL15@P0: 5448 1958 Pan [16]\nL16@P0: 777 1965 Chen [1]\nL17@P0: 630 1971 Jutila [17, p. 370]\nL18@P0: 550 1970 Jutila [10]\nL19@P0: 168 1977 Chen [2]\nL20@P0: 80 1977 Jutila [11]\nL21@P0: 36 1977 Graham [6]\nL22@P0: 20 1981 Graham [7]\nL23@P0: 17 1979 Chen [3]\nL24@P0: 16 1986 Wang [18]\nL25@P0: 13.5 1989 Chen and Liu [4]\nL26@P0: 11.5 1991 Chen and Liu [5]\nL27@P0: 8 1991 Wang [19]\nL28@P0: 5.5 1992 Heath-Brown [8]\nL29@P0: 2010 Mathematics Subject Classification: Primary 11N13; Secondary 11M06.\nL30@P0: Key words and phrases: least prime in an arithmetic progression, Linnik’s theorem, Lin\u0002nik’s constant, Dirichlet L-functions, zero-free regions, zero-density estimates.\nL31@P0: DOI: 10.4064/aa150-1-4 [65]\nL32@P0-1: c Instytut Matematyczny PAN, 201166 T. Xylouris\nL33@P1: In [8, pp. 332–337] Heath-Brown mentions several small suggestions for\nL34@P1: improvement to his work. Making use of these suggestions we prove\nL35@P1: Theorem 1.1. We have\nL36@P1: P(a, q) ≤ Cq5.18\nL37@P1: with an effectively computable constant C.\nL38@P1: In the course of the proof of Theorem 1.1 we improve on several results\nL39@P1: in [8] concerning zero-free regions and zero-density estimates for Dirichlet\nL40@P1: L-functions. Let us cite, for example, the following result.\nL41@P1: Theorem 1.2. There is an effectively computable constant q0, such that\nL42@P1: for q ≥ q0 the function\nL43@P1: Y\nL44@P1: χ (mod q)\nL45@P1: L(s, χ)\nL46@P1: has at most one zero in the region\nL47@P1: σ ≥ 1 −\nL48@P1: 0.440\nL49@P1: log q\nL50@P1: , |t| ≤ 1.\nL51@P1: If this exceptional zero (“Siegel zero”) exists then it is real, simple and be\u0002longs to a non-principal real character.\nL52@P1: Remark. Heath-Brown [8, Theorem 1, p. 268] has proved this estimate\nL53@P1: with c = 0.348 instead of c = 0.440. By a small variation of Heath-Brown’s\nL54@P1: argument, Liu and Wang [14, pp. 345–346] achieved c = 0.364.\nL55@P1: For a motivation as well as a more detailed introduction into this topic\nL56@P1: we refer to [9, §18], [8, pp. 265–270] and [20, pp. 7–10].\nL57@P1: In this paper we refine certain arguments of [8] in the way proposed\nL58@P1: by the nine improvement suggestions in [8, pp. 332–337]. To be precise,\nL59@P1: we use suggestions (2), (5), (7) and (9), the largest contribution to our\nL60@P1: improved estimates being due to suggestion (2). On the other hand, the\nL61@P1: improvement of the admissible value for L resulting from our use of the\nL62@P1: other five suggestions is too small and will not be discussed here.\nL63@P1: Besides some small variations, this paper is a shorter version of our work\nL64@P1: [20] and the reader is referred to that work whenever more details are desired.\nL65@P1: Standard notation from analytic number theory is used. For q ∈ N =\nL66@P1: {1, 2, 3, . . .} we use χ to denote a Dirichlet character modulo q, χ0 for the\nL67@P1: principal character modulo q and L(s, χ) to denote the corresponding Dirich\u0002let L-function. Furthermore, we use ord χ for the order of χ in the group of\nL68@P1: Dirichlet characters modulo q, and the notation [x] = max{a ∈ Z | a ≤ x}\nL69@P1: and\nL70@P1-2: L = log q.The least prime in an arithmetic progression 67\nL71@P2: The real part of a complex number z is denoted by <{z} and its imaginary\nL72@P2: part by ={z}. We refer the unfamiliar reader to the detailed explanations\n"
